{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"royal-weariless-zebrafish","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Report\n\nWhen a store write cannot acquire the write lock within its budget, the write is DISCARDED. There is no retry, no spool, and no adoption by a later pass. The CLI raises; best-effort emitters swallow it.\n\nMeasured during the 7084 lock diagnosis, against a read-only clone of the real store: **one long lock holder plus six concurrent `rebar comment`s produced 6/6 failures at exactly 60s, and ALL SIX WRITES WERE PERMANENTLY LOST** — `rebar show` afterwards contained none of them. An unrelated `claim` died at 30s because `txn.py:87` passes `attempts=1`.\n\nIndependently reproduced in the field the same day: a `rebar comment` failed with `flock: could not acquire lock after 60s` and its content was gone; the caller had to notice and retype it.\n\n## Why the current shape is wrong for this store\n\n* **Budgets are inverted relative to the work they guard.** 60s for a ~0.13s comment; 60s for a rename loop; **30s for `claim`**, the most contended verb; 15s for push/sync merges.\n* **Waiters are a lottery, not a queue.** Both acquisition legs non-blocking-poll with jitter, so there is no fairness and P(timeout) rises with writer count — adding agents makes LOST WRITES more likely, not merely slower.\n* **Expiry is terminal.** For an APPEND-ONLY event store this is the wrong failure shape. Latency under contention is acceptable; silent data loss is not.\n\n## Relationship to 7084 (now closed)\n\n7084 fixed lock HOLD DURATION — the profiled ticket close went from 48.1s to ~1.0s (R1), the holder is now named on timeout (R5), and compact-on-close stands aside when the lock is busy (R3). That removes the dominant *cause* of contention but does NOT change what happens when a writer still loses the race. This ticket owns that half.\n\nPost-fix re-measurement under the same 6-writer harness has NOT been rerun. It is possible that at ~1s holds the timeout is now unreachable in practice — that measurement is the first thing to do here, and it may downgrade the priority. It does not make the failure shape correct.\n\n## Prior consideration\n\nThis was ranked as R6 in the 7084 diagnosis (\"spool-and-adopt on LockTimeout so loss becomes latency\") and was explicitly NOT approved at the time, because it introduces 'phantom write' semantics — a write that is accepted but not yet visible — which is a product decision, not a mechanical fix. That decision is still open and belongs here.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] Re-measure the 6-writer contention harness against post-R1/R3/R5 main and record whether writes are still lost. If they are not, say so with numbers and rescope this ticket rather than building for a problem that no longer occurs\n- [ ] If loss is still reachable, a decision is recorded on the failure shape: spool-and-adopt (loss becomes latency, with phantom-write semantics stated and bounded), fair queueing, per-operation budgets proportional to measured hold time, or an explicit accepted-loss policy\n- [ ] Whatever is chosen, a write that cannot be committed is never SILENTLY discarded — the caller learns, in a way that survives the best-effort emitters that currently swallow it\n- [ ] Budgets are reconciled against measured hold times; `claim`'s 30s/`attempts=1` is justified or corrected\n- [ ] A test reproduces the loss and proves the chosen remedy","id":"10c3-5a33-2e25-4584","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"A write whose lock acquire times out is PERMANENTLY LOST — the budget is terminal and lossy, not a queue"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1786127215932809001,"uuid":"5d4b2291-ad57-432f-9bff-ce957006a927"}